- How would you stress-test for race conditions during stack growth?
- What safeguards prevent stack overflow when many threads fault concurrently?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of synchronization primitives and memory ordering in a toy OS context.

## Key Concepts

- Barrier reuse via a generation counter
- Race-free wakeups with a condition variable analogue
- Memory visibility and minimal locking
- Testing for progress and fairness under contention

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct {
  int count;
  int needed;
  int gen;
  pthread_mutex_t m;
  pthread_cond_t cv;
} barrier_t;

void barrier_init(barrier_t *b, int n) {
  b->count = 0;
  b->needed = n;
  b->gen = 0;
  pthread_mutex_init(&b->m, NULL);
  pthread_cond_init(&b->cv, NULL);
}

void barrier_wait(barrier_t *b) {
  pthread_mutex_lock(&b->m);
  int g = b->gen;
  if (++b->count == b->needed) {
    b->count = 0;
    b->gen++;
    pthread_cond_broadcast(&b->cv);
  } else {
    while (b->gen == g) {
      pthread_cond_wait(&b->cv, &b->m);
    }
  }
  pthread_mutex_unlock(&b->m);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this for dynamic participant counts?
- How would you test for spurious wakeups and ensure progress under contention?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of IRQ-safe producer-consumer in a tiny kernel.

## Key Concepts

- IRQ-safe synchronization
- Ring buffer wraparound
- Blocking vs non-blocking reads
- Memory barriers and visibility

## Code Example

```javascript
typedef struct {
  uint8_t buf[16];
  volatile uint8_t head;
  volatile uint8_t tail;
} ring_t;

void isr_write(ring_t *r, uint8_t v){
  uint8_t next = (r->head + 1) & 15;
  if (next == r->tail) return; // overflow
  r->buf[r->head] = v;
  __sync_synchronize();
  r->head = next;
  // wake reader if blocked
}

int read_user(ring_t *r, uint8_t *out){
  while (r->head == r->tail) {
    // block or yield
  }
  *out = r->buf[r->tail];
  r->tail = (r->tail + 1) & 15;
  return 1;
}
```

