## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of mmap, page cache, locking, and cross-process synchronization when file size changes.

## Key Concepts
- MAP_SHARED semantics
- Page cache invalidation and TLB coherence
- Synchronization patterns: locks, munmap/remap, msync
- Truncation behavior and SIGBUS vs stale data

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example: pseudo C-like code showing lock around ftruncate and remap
int fd = open("data.bin", O_RDWR);
pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;
pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
void *p = mmap(NULL, 4096, PROT_READ|PROT_WRITE, MAP_SHARED, fd, 0);
// reader uses p...
// Truncator later:
ftruncate(fd, 2048); // shorten file
munmap(p, 4096);
// remap after truncation
p = mmap(NULL, 2048, PROT_READ|PROT_WRITE, MAP_SHARED, fd, 0);
pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this pattern for IPC via memory-mapped files?
- What changes on Windows for similar semantics?

